op.c: S_maybe_multideref counts from a real base, not by incrementing NULL.

The function runs twice: the first pass counts the multideref arguments by
incrementing `arg` from `arg_buf`, which is NULL on that pass, and takes the
count as `arg - arg_buf`. Every write through `arg` is guarded by `if (pass)`, so
the pass stores nothing -- it walks a null pointer as a counter. That is
undefined in C and harmless on an ordinary machine. Here pointer arithmetic is
capability arithmetic and cincoffset on an untagged register traps: cause 24 in
S_maybe_multideref, reached by any subscript chain, so `$h{k}` in a script is
enough. Measured at -O2 and again at -O1 (2026-09-26), which is what says it is
the program rather than the optimiser hoisting.

The pass is given a valid base of the right type instead, a single local item.
The count is unchanged, nothing is stored through it, and arg_buf is replaced by
the real allocation before the second pass. The pointer does leave that local's
bounds while counting; this ISA allows arithmetic out of bounds and refuses
arithmetic without authority, and no load or store happens through it.

Under __CAPSTONE_PURECAP__ only, so every other platform keeps perl's own code.

--- a/op.c	2026-09-26 15:26:56.929911268 +0800
+++ b/op.c	2026-09-26 15:26:56.942226666 +0800
@@ -16416,6 +16416,23 @@
 {
     int pass;
     UNOP_AUX_item *arg_buf = NULL;
+#ifdef __CAPSTONE_PURECAP__
+    /* capstone: the first pass counts the arguments by walking a pointer from
+       arg_buf -- which is NULL then -- and takes the count as `arg - arg_buf`
+       below. `arg++` on a null pointer is undefined in C and harmless on an
+       ordinary machine; here pointer arithmetic is capability arithmetic, and
+       cincoffset on an untagged register traps (cause 24 in S_maybe_multideref,
+       reached by any subscript chain -- `$h{k}` in a script is enough; measured
+       2026-09-26 at -O2 and at -O1, so it is the program and not the optimiser).
+       A valid base of the right type is all the pass needs: every write through
+       arg is under `if (pass)`, so nothing is stored, and only the difference is
+       read. The pointer does leave the dummy's bounds, which this ISA allows for
+       arithmetic -- what it refuses is arithmetic without authority, and a load
+       or store out of bounds, neither of which happens here. arg_buf is replaced
+       by the real allocation before the second pass. */
+    UNOP_AUX_item arg_count_base;
+    arg_buf = &arg_count_base;
+#endif
     bool reset_start_targ  = FALSE; /* start->op_targ needs zeroing */
     int index_skip         = -1;    /* don't output index arg on this action */
 
